        "source_handle": "P0354",
        "field_path": [
          "study_summary_A",
          "problem_or_question"
        ],
        "value": "Previous studies on the gut microbiome and ICI response have yielded inconsistent results due to variable analytic methods, small sample sizes, and differing bioinformatics pipelines. This study aims to determine if a standardized computational approach can reveal robust, generalizable microbial biomarkers for ICI outcomes across different cancer types and geographic locations, specifically focusing on identifying signals for both responders and non-responders.",
        "role": "evidence",
        "snapshot_ids": [
          "snapshot::a7a7de9fbd4946fc2daa9ddf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "atom_id": "atom::0178b697c366cb2c1b5339eb",
        "source_handle": "P0354",
        "field_path": [
          "study_summary_A",
          "research_scope"
        ],
        "value": "The study focuses on the gut microbiome as a biomarker for predicting clinical outcomes (response vs. non-response) in patients with solid tumors (melanoma, NSCLC, RCC, HCC) treated with immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs). It reanalyzes raw 16S rRNA and metagenomic sequencing data from five previously published cohorts (n=303 unique patients) and validates findings in three independent cohorts (n=105).",
        "role": "evidence",
        "snapshot_ids": [
          "snapshot::a7a7de9fbd4946fc2daa9ddf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "atom_id": "atom::d845e1afbea13aaf3976bcf2",
        "source_handle": "P0354",
        "field_path": [
          "study_summary_A",
          "work_summary"
        ],
        "value": "This empirical study addresses the inconsistency in microbial biomarkers for ICI efficacy across previous small-scale studies by applying a uniform computational pipeline to reanalyze raw sequencing data from multiple cohorts. The authors identified novel bacterial signatures associated with responders (R) and non-responders (NR), developed an integrated microbiome prediction index, and found that signals associated with non-response were stronger and more consistent than those for response. The study highlights the potential of NR-associated indices to identify patients who might benefit from microbiome-based interventions.",
        "role": "evidence",
        "snapshot_ids": [
          "snapshot::a7a7de9fbd4946fc2daa9ddf"
        ]
      },
